artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $P_n$ be a set of $n$ points in general position in the plane (no three collinear), and let $\operatorname{conv}(P_n)$ denote its convex hull. A convex decomposition of $\operatorname{conv}(P_n)$ with respect to $P_n$ is a finite family $\mathcal{F}$ of convex polygons such that:
(i) the polygons in $\mathcal{F}$ have pairwise disjoint interiors;
(ii) the union of the polygons in $\mathcal{F}$ equals $\operatorname{conv}(P_n)$;
(iii) the set of vertices of polygons in $\mathcal{F}$ is exactly $P_n$;
(iv) no polygon of $\mathcal{F}$ contains a point of $P_n$ in its interior.

Decide whether the following statement is true: for every $n$ and every set $P_n$ of $n$ points in general position in the plane, there exists such a convex decomposition $\mathcal{F}$ with $|\mathcal{F}| \le n+1$.

